<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Area of a region above and below the x-axis</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the curve \(y = {@yx@}\) for \(0 \le x \le {@hh@}\).</p><p>(a) Find the \(x\)-coordinate, other than \(0\), where the curve crosses the \(x\)-axis.</p><div>\(x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the exact value of \(\displaystyle \int_0^{ {@hh@} } y\,dx\).</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the exact total area between the curve and the \(x\)-axis from \(x = 0\) to \(x = {@hh@}\).</p><div>Area \(=\) [[input:ans3]] square units [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Sketch first. The curve crosses the \(x\)-axis inside the interval, so part of the region is above the axis and part below. The definite integral counts the part below as negative; the area adds the sizes of the parts.</p><p>(a) \(y = {@mm*x*(x-rr)@}\), so \(y = 0\) when \(x = 0\) or \(x = {@rr@}\).</p><p>(b) \[\int_0^{ {@hh@} } \left({@yx@}\right)dx = \left[{@Yx@}\right]_0^{ {@hh@} } = {@net@}\]</p><p>(c) \(\displaystyle \int_0^{ {@rr@} } y\,dx = {@I1@}\) and \(\displaystyle \int_{ {@rr@} }^{ {@hh@} } y\,dx = {@I2@}\). Adding the sizes, the total area is \({@A1@} + {@A2@} = {@tot@}\) square units.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* Design: regions above and below the axis. [m, r, h]: y = m x^2 - m r x on [0, h], crossing at x = r; 5 entries with m > 0, 5 with m < 0. No board: it would show r. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 1, chapter 11, Exercise D question 4. */
L: [[-6,1,3],[-1,3,6],[3/2,3,4],[-3/2,4,5],[-3/2,5,8],[3/2,5,6],[-3/2,6,7],[3/2,6,8],[3/2,2,6],[6,1,2]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
mm: L[ix][1]; rr: L[ix][2]; hh: L[ix][3];
yx: mm*x^2-mm*rr*x;
Yx: integrate(yx,x);
net: subst(x=hh,Yx);
I1: subst(x=rr,Yx); I2: net-I1;
A1: abs(I1); A2: abs(I2);
tot: A1+A2;
/* diagnostics */
absnet: abs(net);]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
  <specificfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></specificfeedback>
  <questionnote format="moodle_auto_format">
    <text>ix={@ix@}, m={@mm@}, r={@rr@}, h={@hh@}, net={@net@}, total={@tot@}</text>
  </questionnote>
  <questiondescription format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p id="concept">Integration: area of a region above and below the x-axis</p><p id="description">Grade 12 CBE Core 4.2: Find where a curve crosses the x-axis, the net definite integral, and the total area of regions above and below the axis.</p><p id="edition">Core</p><p id="provenance">This question is adapted from Exercise D, Question 4 in Chapter 11 of Cassell, D., Instone, S., Ling, J., Scruton, P., Shilton, S. and West, H., SMP AS/A2 Mathematics: Core 1 for AQA, Cambridge University Press, 2004: new quadratic curves and intervals, contrasting the net integral with the total area.</p><p id="citation">INNODEMS CBC Grade 12 Mathematics, first draft by Ben Davies, 2026. Aligned to the KICD Senior School Curriculum Design, Core Mathematics Grade 12 (draft).</p>]]></text></questiondescription>
